#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216929 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "54d19544-7156-58e9-a090-12f8e9492226" :knowledge-point/title "Testing Integrating Factors for ODEs in Differential Form"}] :question/id #uuid "b32d0028-8e5c-5f8a-b989-a7ceff5c1fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-20896" :question/problem "Show that $I(x) = \\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ is an integrating factor of the equation\n$(x^2y-y^3) \\,\\textrm{d}x - x^{3} \\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y] = 0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{xy^{3}}((x^{2}y - y^{3})dx - x^{3}dy) &= 0 \\\\\n(\\frac{x}{y^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x})dx - \\frac{x^{2}}{y^{3}}dy &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y^2}-\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad Q(x,y) =-\\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x}) =-\\frac{2x}{y^{3}} \\\\\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{x^{2}}{y^{3}}) =-\\frac{2x}{y^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\dfrac{1}{xy^3}\n$$\n\n$\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216930 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a016197-61af-57f3-9c3a-05410db1374d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inexact Differential Equations Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "106cd4dd-2f3f-541e-899a-e2776a39e382" :question/math-academy-id "e-20898" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$\\left(x + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\left(xy + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n$P(x,y)=x+y^2,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{2}{x}$.\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x$.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{∫x^{-1}dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln x} \\\\\n&= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx[(x + y^{2})dx + (xy + \\frac{2}{x})dy] &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + xy^{2})_(⏟)_(P^(~))dx + (x^{2}y + 2)_(⏟)_(Q^(~))dy &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad \\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x$.\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y$, which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫P^(~)(x, y)dx + F(y) \\\\\n&= ∫(x^{2} + xy^{2})dx + F(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{2} + F(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y$.\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x$, which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫Q^(~)(x, y)dy + G(x) \\\\\n&= ∫(x^{2}y + 2)dy + G(x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{2} + 2y + G(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216931 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cbedeed-6ac6-5ed8-bd25-9a85eda9577b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integrating Factor for an Inexact Equation: Condition on X Satisfied"}] :question/id #uuid "b69fa09b-2661-5edc-8fcb-c29120e56325" :question/math-academy-id "e-20935" :question/problem "Find an integrating factor for the following equation in differential form.\n$x\\,y' = 4y - 3x$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x) = \\exp\\left({\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form:\n\n$$\n(3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + (x) \\;\\textrm{d} y =0\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 3x - 4y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x - 4y) =-4 \\\\\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}x = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) &= \\frac{1}{x}(-4 - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= \\exp (∫ - \\frac{5}{x}dx) \\\\\n&= \\exp (-5\\ln \\mid x \\mid) \\\\\n&= x^{-5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x) \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) = I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx^{-5} \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216952 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "161b35e2-52bf-5f27-9fc3-bfe34eeb7935" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integrating Factor for an Inexact Equation: Condition on Y Satisfied"}] :question/id #uuid "294fc38f-1d72-52ed-a11d-d1003fb1b1bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-21772" :question/problem "Find an integrating factor for the following differential equation.\n\n$y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y = 0, \\qquad y > 0$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{P} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y) = \\exp\\left({-\\int h(y)\\,\\textrm d y}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^y$.\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^2, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2}) = 2y \\\\\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) = y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) &= \\frac{1}{y^{2}}(2y - y) \\\\\n&= y^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(y) &= \\exp (∫ - (y^{-1})dy) \\\\\n&= \\exp (-\\ln y) \\\\\n&= y^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot \\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy\\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y^{-1}\\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = 0}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a016197-61af-57f3-9c3a-05410db1374d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inexact Differential Equations Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e958fa7a-2530-5e28-9195-e3dd1010f6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216568} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1ef443-4597-541d-a8b8-ab8eac9b4807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216569} {:answer/id #uuid "331ca236-b1f1-5b54-9bf6-4ef67cedef79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216570} {:answer/id #uuid "64939ccc-a9bb-5407-afbc-0c31373b00bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216571}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7b6ed42b-5c85-5ddb-bee9-efa742312304" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f23ef2ff-c615-5327-8856-dcaefed546c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2}y^{4} + 3x^{2}y^{2} + xy = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216573} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9098f4-a6d7-59b2-aa95-8e2743fc9f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{4}y^{3} + 2x^{3}y^{2} + y = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216574} {:answer/id #uuid "139036de-8f75-5274-bd83-8a15afa69a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}y^{3} + 2xy^{2} + y = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216575} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffffdcd-1009-542a-b618-7670fe4f7c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{2} + xy^{2} + xy = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216576}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5ccc11c6-641b-5db3-a990-fccf006a1b53" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216572}] :question/id #uuid "1effee9c-dd60-525b-b725-817b354982d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337096" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$(\\frac{6y^{2}}{x} + 4y^{3})dx + (4y + 3xy^{2} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})dy = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) = \\frac{3}{x}$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$P(x, y) = \\frac{6y^{2}}{x} + 4y^{3}, Q(x, y) = 4y + 3xy^{2} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}}$.\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\nA suitable integrating factor is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "54d19544-7156-58e9-a090-12f8e9492226" :knowledge-point/title "Testing Integrating Factors for ODEs in Differential Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8a5ec6e-382e-583d-aa39-e3c4ed4dd035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y)/(x^{2})dx − (y^{2})/(x^{3})dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216588} {:answer/id #uuid "df3f849a-11e4-504a-81c3-39dfe079cc47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x^{2})/(y)dx − (x^{3})/(y^{2})dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216589} {:answer/id #uuid "df7e1f30-615b-5d7b-a742-8e4a07fecf07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (x^{3})/(y^{2})dx − (x^{2})/(y)dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216590} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc30822-71a6-5178-8cde-e735a60b192e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (y^{2})/(x^{3})dx + (y)/(x^{2})dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216591}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c4b08c43-96b5-597e-9a79-d2a69a0b610e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216587}] :question/id #uuid "a9a8802d-3d4d-5564-8390-a80c9704bb8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337133" :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$xdy - ydx = 0$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(x, y) = \\frac{y}{x^{3}}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that $I(x, y) = \\frac{y}{x^{3}}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$-\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{3}}dx + \\frac{y}{x^{2}}dy$ {{field-1}}"}]}
